  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 153 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow... Rain continues to fall across much of\nsouthern Minnesota and western Wisconsin this afternoon. This\narea of stratiform rain will gradually shift to the north this\nafternoon into the evening. Some more isolated to scattered rain\nshowers could come together south of this main area of rain this\nevening into tonight. This is all driven by a shortwave off to\nour west. The PVA induced synoptic lift combined with warm and\nmoist southerly flow at 850 mb has given us a solid rain setup.\nThis flow has driven precipitable water values up above the \n90th percentile both in observed climatology based on the 12Z \nMPX sounding this morning and the NAEFS analysis. In addition, \nall this saturation has also kept us in cloud cover. These\nclouds will persist well into tomorrow. This cloud cover has \nhelped to keep temperatures low today with highs struggling to \nget out of the 40s. On the other side, temperatures will not \nfall much tonight due to these same clouds. Temperatures \ntonight will also be buoyed by some WAA. As the primary forcing \nshifts north some rain chances will continue across central \nMinnesota into tomorrow morning. Tomorrow looks to be warmer as \nWAA occurs with highs in the 50s to some lower 60s south of I-94\nand east of I-35. \n\nWednesday to Thursday... The early week WAA will continue as we\nmove into Wednesday. This will set Wednesday up to be the \nwarmest day of the week. Highs generally in the 60s, but lower \n70s are also likely (most likely across southern MN and western \nWI). This warm up won't last long as a cold front will move \nthrough late Wednesday. This cold frontal passage will also be \nthe driver for our next round of rain. This should be a quicker \nround of rain as it will be forced by this front rather than \ntoday's broader scale forcing. This means by Thursday morning \nrain chances will be falling off quickly and some of the\nlingering PoPs are more based on some timing differences within\nensemble guidance. With the warmer airmass ahead of the front \nthere could be enough instability for some thunderstorms on \nWednesday ahead of the frontal passage. The modest instability\nwill make it unlikely for deep convection and therefore severe\nstorms to occur. Looking towards Thursday's temperatures, this \npost-frontal airmass will be cooler than Wednesday. Due to high\npressure clearing the skies though, temperatures will still be \nabove normal with high temperatures in the mid 50s to mid 60s on\nThursday.\n\nFriday into the weekend... Friday looks fairly similar to\nThursday with the exception of the clouds starting to return\nlate as the next system starts to move in from the west. Still a\nfair amount of spread in how this system plays out this weekend,\nso NBM PoPs remain broad and low. In general, when comparing \nthe global ensemble systems southern Minnesota has the highest \nchance with more membership giving rain chances than locations \nto the north. As we move into the middle of this week there \nshould be more convergence in the various members of the \nensembles for us to gain more confidence in where this round of \nrain will occur. The overall movement of this system though does\nlook to bring back the southerly flow and above normal \ntemperatures look to continue through the weekend. Looking \nfarther ahead into long range GEFS guidance it could be well \ninto November before MSP Airport, the Twin Cities official \nclimate site, falls below freezing. The record latest freezing\ntemperature is November 18th from 2016, so still a while to go.\nGEFS means though show that it could be close to or after this \nrecord when MSP finally falls below freezing.",
